syntax: zsh applies ${#name} to the result of a further operator

In zsh, the length prefix combines with every other expansion operator and
counts what that operator produces, so zsh -n accepts, and zsh expands,

	x=abcabc
	echo ${#x%c} ${#x/a/zz} ${#x:-q}   # 5 7 6
	(( ${#words[@]:#-*} > 1 ))

Completion functions and prompt themes use it to count the elements left
after a filter, as in ${#PREFIX%$hash*} or ${(M)#words:#*=*}. The parser
rejected all of them with "cannot combine multiple parameter expansion
operators".

With LangZsh, a ParamExp may now have Length set together with Exp, Repl,
Slice or Modifiers. Bash, mksh and ksh93 still reject the combination, as
they do when the expansion runs.

Reproducer, before this change: `echo ${#x%c}` with LangZsh failed with
"cannot combine multiple parameter expansion operators".

